  "worked_solution": "We first write $25{r}^{3}$ as the product $25{r}^{2}\\cdot r$ which contains a perfect square $(25{r}^{2}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{25{r}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{25{r}^{2}\\cdot r} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can separate the product into two separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{25{r}^{2}\\cdot r} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(5r)^{2}\\cdot r} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(5r)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{r} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the radical containing the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(5r)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{r} & = \\\\ |5r|\\cdot \\sqrt{r} & = \\\\ |5|\\cdot |r|\\cdot \\sqrt{r} & = \\\\ 5|r|\\sqrt{r} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
